-- title:
--   Diamond operator realised as Picard transport on the Jacobian model
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. A prime $p$ and an integer $M$ with $M \neq 0$, $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$ ($hM$, $hpM$); a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $\{p\}$, together with an element $\zeta \in L$ that is a primitive $p$-th root of unity ($h\zeta$); an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which by $hK$ equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; a discrete valuation ring $A$ which is a domain with fraction field $L$, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ ($hAp$) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ ($h\zeta A$); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \subseteq L \subseteq K$; and an element $j \in K$ whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficient embedding of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) ($hj$), with $j \neq 0$.
--
--   Geometric data over $A$. Write $c =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) for the structure morphism of the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper. There is a section $\varepsilon$ of $c$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation $D$, that is, a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase} \colon D.P \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and a section $D.\mathrm{zeroSection}$. The hypothesis $hrep$ asserts that $D$ represents, with Poincaré bundle and universal property, the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor of $(c, \varepsilon)$ cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut`, whose value on a rigidified line bundle is that its restriction to every geometric fibre is algebraically equivalent to zero; $hsm$ and $hsep$ require $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to be smooth and separated. Compatible $A$- and $L$-algebra structures on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` are fixed. The base change of $c$ along $A \to L$ is assumed smooth of relative dimension $1$ ($hsmL$) and geometrically integral ($hgiL$), and the projection `pullback.snd D.toBase (specMap A L)` is assumed proper ($hprL$) and geometrically connected ($hgcL$).
--
--   The geometric fibre as a curve model. $M\eta$ is a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), i.e. a proper integral scheme, smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with a ring isomorphism of that field onto its function field compatible with the base field, and a bijection between its closed points and the places of the field. The morphism $e\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M\eta.C$ onto the pullback of $c$ along $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, compatible with the structure morphisms ($he\eta$). The preimage under $e\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart is assumed nonempty, and $hM\eta pin$ pins the identification of function fields on that chart: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135), the element of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) obtained by transporting, through $M\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, the germ at the generic point of the section determined by $a$ has underlying Laurent series equal to the image of the Laurent series of $a$ under the coefficient map induced by $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The hypothesis $hgal$ is the Galois equivariance of the point–place dictionary: for every $g \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta$ over $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, if the chart composite of $x'$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $g$ followed by that of $x$, then $M\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathrm{arithmeticGalois}(\mathrm{x1FunctionField}(Mp))(g) \cdot M\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$.
--
--   Hecke and Galois inputs. $hin$ is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58), asserting the Hecke inputs at every prime together with, for every $d$ coprime to $Mp$, the existence of a diamond automorphism of `x1FunctionField (M * p)` and of a base change of it to `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`; $hcomm$ is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), the commutation of all the Hecke and diamond generators acting on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-divisor classes. Moreover $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q}) = (L \simeq_{\mathbb{Q}} L)$ acts on $A$ by semiring automorphisms, compatibly with $A \to L$ ($h\Gamma A$).
--
--   The points dictionary and Abel–Jacobi data. $gpts$ is a bijection from [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the group of degree-zero divisor classes of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` modulo principal divisors, onto the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to D.P$ over $D.\mathrm{toBase}$; $hgadd$ asserts that $gpts$ is additive for the relative group law on $D$ supplied by $hrep$. Over $L$: $hDL$ asserts that $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L$ represents the corresponding subfunctor for the base-changed curve and section; $ajL$ is a morphism from the base-changed curve to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} L$; $kL$ is a comparison morphism from the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-pullback of $c$ to the $L$-pullback, constrained by $hkL_1$ and $hkL_2$ to commute with the first projections and to intertwine the second projections with $\operatorname{Spec} L \to \operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; $hPL$ identifies the Poincaré bundle of $hDL$ with the base change along $L$ of the Poincaré bundle of $hrep$ pulled back along the first projection; $hajL\varepsilon$ says that $ajL$ sends the base-changed section to the zero section; and $hajL$ is the Abel–Jacobi property of $ajL$: for every field $K'$, every $t \colon \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every point $x$ of the base-changed curve over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $ajL$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section at $t$. On the geometric fibre, $ajbar \colon M\eta.C \to D.P$ is prescribed by $hajbar$ as $e\eta$ followed by $kL$, by $ajL$ and by the first projection, lies over the base ($hajbar\_over$), $\bar\varepsilon$ is the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M\eta$ induced by $\varepsilon$ ($h\bar\varepsilon$) and is sent by $ajbar$ to the zero section ($h\bar\varepsilon\_aj$). Finally $hpts\_aj$ pins $gpts$ by Abel–Jacobi: for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $s$ of $M\eta$ with $s$ the point induced by $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ equal to $[\,\text{place of }x\,] - [\,\text{place of }s\,]$ whose class satisfies $(gpts(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of } Dv)).1 = x$ followed by $ajbar$.
--
--   The diamond data. An integer $d$ coprime to $Mp$ ($hd$); an $L$-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of $K$ which, under the identification of $K$ with [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) on the level of underlying Laurent series, agrees with the base change to $L$ of the diamond automorphism [`ModularCurve.diamondAut (M * p) d`](def/ModularCurve_X1Diamond.html#L66) ($h\theta$); an automorphism $w$ of the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ ($hw$) whose restriction to the finite chart is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of a ring automorphism $\rho$ of the finite chart algebra ($hw\rho$), where $\rho$ acts as $\theta$ on elements of that algebra ($h\rho$); and an endomorphism $\theta_w$ of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ characterised by $h\theta_w$: for every $T$ with a morphism $t$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, every pair of rigidified line bundles $P_1$, $P_2$ on the curve over $t$ satisfying the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero condition and every invertible module $Q$ on $T$, if $P_2.L$ is isomorphic to the pullback of $P_1.L$ along `curveChange w.hom hw t` tensored with the pullback of $Q$ along the second projection, then composing the classifying morphism of $P_1$ with $\theta_w$ gives the classifying morphism of $P_2$.
--
--   Conclusion. With the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) of the Hecke algebra on [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) in force, for every $x \in$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the underlying morphism $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to D.P$ of $gpts(\mathrm{diamondGen}\,d \cdot x)$ equals that of $gpts(x)$ followed by $\theta_w$:
--   $$(gpts(\langle d\rangle \cdot x)).1 = (gpts\,x).1 \mathbin{;} \theta_w.1 ,$$
--   where $\mathrm{diamondGen}\,d$ is the generator of the Hecke algebra indexed by $d$.
--
--   This is the comparison, on the Jacobian model $D \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ of $X_1(Mp)$, between the action of the diamond operator $\langle d \rangle$ on degree-zero divisor classes of the geometric function field and the Picard transport $\theta_w$ attached to the diamond automorphism $w$ of the two-chart model: the Abel–Jacobi-pinned dictionary $gpts$ between $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$ intertwines the two. It feeds the statements that construct the Hecke and Galois actions on the model and that descend the diamond operators to the special fibre.
